Fracture toughness tests were conducted using a multifunctional testing machine (MTS 370.10, 100 kN, MTS, USA) on specimens with dimensions of 10 × 20 × 100 mm. High-temperature tensile tests were conducted using a multifunctional testing machine (MTS 370.10, 100 KN, MTS, USA) equipped with a high-temperature environmental furnace. The high-temperature tensile tests were performed at temperatures ranging from 100 to 600 °C, with strain control applied until the maximum load was reached. At that point, the sample was subjected to displacement control applied until the sample fractured. The strain rate and displacement rate were 2.5 × 10 −4 /s and 6.25 × 10 −3  mm/s, respectively. The strain variation after exceeding the maximum load was calculated by fitting a linear relationship between displacement and strain prior to reaching the maximum load. These specimen dimensions were also cylindrical, 65 mm long and 3 mm in diameter, with a gauge length of 15 mm. The mathematical description of maximum elongation at fracture (FE) and reduction in area (RA) can be expressed by the following equations: 1 FE = ( L F − L O ) / L O × 100 % \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
